      "content": "Originalism is a legitimate constitutional method because it strictly adheres to the text and original public meaning, which is the only way to ensure the law remains stable and predictable across generations. While critics claim it protects entrenched interests, the core argument is that it prevents the judiciary from acting as a \"super-legislature\" by refusing to update laws based on modern political whims. If the goal is to protect the rule of law and limit judicial overreach, then originalism is the only viable framework, regardless of how inconvenient it may be for certain policy goals.",
      "source": "target",
